  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...High pressure builds in from the west through \nmost of this week with sunny skies. Building hts as upper ridge\nstrengthens sends temps soaring through the 80s and nearing 90\nfor some interior locales (record high climate section below). \nA back door front moves into nrn parts of ENC later Thur with\nsome increasing cloud cover and slightly cooler temps behind \nit, but quickly retreats north by early Friday with another \nsurge of swrly flow ahead of much stronger front. No moisture \nwith the backdoor front, and only temporary reductions in temps \nfor nrn 1/3 of ENC. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A trend towards a slower frontal arrival and\nprogression, with low pres development along the frontal zone on\nSaturday. Followed closely to the ECM, which has been setting\nthis trend over the last several runs, with the 14/12Z\ncontinuing this wet trend with high pops, but rather low QPF\nwhich is typical with ana fronts, where stratiform rain follows\nthe sfc front. GFS is now an outlier, with most of the global\nmodel suite on board with the wetter trend, so it has been\ndiscounted. Cold front finally passes through the coast around\n12Z Sat morning, thus Fri night still quite mild in swrly flow.\nNon-diurnal temp trend Sat with temps cooling through the day\nwith rain, clouds, and strengthening nrly winds. \n\nRegarding the winds, model trends have also trended upwards with\nwinds, with gales in frequent gusts now likely for most marine\nzones including the sounds, and favorably aligned Alligator\nRiver, as ECM probs for these gales now over 80%. Winds adv\ncriteria fcst for the OBX zones, as well as perhaps portions of\nthe adjacent coastal mainland. Will have to watch for minor\ncoastal flood concerns for srn portions of Pamlico Sound and the\ncoastal areas of the OBX.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Coolest and driest airmass for the fall season \nthus far builds in behind the front Sat night and beyond with \ntemps taking a nosedive with highs in the 60s and lows in the \n40s (inland) and 50s (beaches). However, depending how offshore \nthe front makes it, could keep things a bit milder than fcst if \nsome clouds advect in from offshore keeping the nighttime mins \na bit warmer than advertised.",
